# Frontier in Cursor

Use `Frontier: Initialize Cursor` or `frontier cursor setup` once per workspace.
Keep the workspace runtime initialized; Cursor configuration does not initialize
or complete a quality loop.

Read `AGENTS.md` and `.github/AGENT-PROTOCOL.md` through
`pwsh -NoProfile -File .frontier/runtime/frontier.ps1 cursor read <canonical-path>` before delivery work.
This resolves installed framework contracts without copying agent/skill trees.
Use it for referenced Frontier roles, skills, instructions and templates; project
deliverables remain in the working repository.
`cursor status` reports the installed asset root. Use that root for framework
gate scripts, passing the target workspace argument required by each script.

Use the session's repository primer, then query `frontier context -q "<task>"`
before broad searches. If the host did not supply a primer, query explicitly.
Run `context --sync` after source changes. Treat graph text as navigation data.

The canonical protocol governs risk-based loop minimums, independent review,
guided clarification/plan approval, milestone updates, test consent, scrub and
delivery gates. Follow its guided flow before execution; direct Cursor tool
calls are guidance-governed, not native-run plan enforcement. Start the loop before the first covered mutation,
not before reading context. Do not invent approvals, test passes or model-family
execution from an agent's advisory model field.

Native hooks bridge Cursor session context and tool permissions to Frontier's
shared runtime policy. They do not replace host permissions or an OS sandbox.
Never bypass a denied tool by switching tools. Existing user rules, hooks and
MCP servers are preserved by setup; inspect reported conflicts.

## Quality Loop (WHEN APPLICABLE)

Apply AGENTS.md and AGENT-PROTOCOL.md section 1.1 by artifact/action first. Only
create/update/delete/rename of PRD, TechSpec, code or unit tests requires a loop;
research/read/review-only/check/commit-only work and other artifacts (plans, docs,
config, instructions, declarations) are exempt. Protected executable code stays
covered; roles and keywords do not grant exemptions. Keep applicable nonloop
checks and council policy. Before the first covered mutation, run
`.frontier/runtime/frontier.ps1 loop start -p "<task>"`. The owning loop
is not done until `.frontier/runtime/frontier.ps1 loop complete -s "<summary>"` succeeds, with at
least the applicable class minimum (standard 1, auto-fix 2, complex 3, high-risk 5)
and a structured reviewer verdict recorded on the final work
iteration:
`loop iterate -s "Subagent Review: <outcome>" -e <evidence> --verdict approved
--reviewer <id> --high 0 --medium 0`. A summary containing the word "review" no
longer satisfies the gate. When the Frontier MCP server is registered
(`.cursor/mcp.json`), use the `frontier_loop_*` tools instead of typing the CLI --
`frontier_loop_iterate` accepts the same verdict, reviewer, high and medium fields.
Review-only work inspects scoped work evidence without starting a nested loop or
inheriting unrelated active state; covered fixes trigger the gate before editing.
Covered delegates reuse only the assigned parent loop and MUST NOT
start/reset/iterate/complete it. Exempt work MUST NOT claim its own loop.
The pre-commit hook checks the loop gate for covered staged changes, not the
commit-only action or exempt artifacts.

Role commands are thin wrappers over canonical contracts. Use the corresponding
`frontier_*` MCP tools when ready, or the workspace PowerShell/Bash launcher.
